{"id":"4689114","slug":"jaxson-dart","name":"Jaxson Dart","position":"QB","team":"NYG","headshot":"https://a.espncdn.com/i/headshots/nfl/players/full/4689114.png","formats":{"ppr-12":{"positionRank":56,"ourRank":81,"vorRound":7,"adp":118.8,"gap":37.8,"projected":252.17,"vor":0,"expectedGames":14.08,"floor":159.17,"median":252.17,"ceiling":345.16,"tier":2,"tierDrop":12.4}},"capsule":{"body":"A rookie first-rounder who finished top three in touches among qualifying quarterbacks last year, right behind Josh Allen, is not a player you wait on out of habit. Dart took 18 red-zone touches and 10 inside the ten in 2025, both top three at the position, and that scoring-range work is why we have him at 252 points and QB12 while drafters keep letting him slide to around pick 119. His ADP has already climbed 9.1 picks since early September, from 119.1 to 110, so the slide is closing on its own. He cleared 15 points in 10 of his 14 weeks as a rookie, so the weekly bar was real, not one hot month, and the best of them hit 28.42 in week 7.\n\nTwo things deepen it. First, the offense: 51% situation-neutral passes and 4 wins as a team, with Matt Nagy calling plays for a new staff, so the run-heavy rookie script is not guaranteed to carry over. The team played at 32.7 seconds a snap, one of the faster tempos on the board, which kept his touch count climbing. Second, and this is the one that matters, he's sitting on IR as of October 13. Our projection counts 14 games, with a realistic range of 8 to 17 once injury risk is in. The low tail of that range comes from seasons cut short by injury, and last year he already missed weeks 11 to 12.\n\nAt ADP 118.8, he's going a round later than he should if the IR stint is short. We would take him a round earlier than that number once there's word on the timeline, and not before. If the IR tag drags into the season, Jameis Winston is the fallback on your roster, not the reason to wait.","pulls":[],"writtenAt":"2026-10-13T22:32:34.219Z"},"weekRea